      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 1:00 p.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Phill Kline)",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This Court has never held that a specific method of weighing aggravating and mitigating evidence is required, yet in its individualized sentencing line of cases this Court has consistently said that all the Eighth Amendment requires is that a juror consider and give effect to all relevant mitigating evidence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: I -- I'm sorry, what instruction is that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Phill Kline)",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0000-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If a juror finds -- that is correct, Justice Stevens -- if the juror's decided conclusion and reasoned moral judgment is that the mitigating factors and the aggravating factors are in balance, and finds that beyond a reasonable doubt, instruction number 10 clearly indicates that death is the appropriate sentence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF REBECCA E. WOODMAN ON BEHALF OF RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Woodman",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0001-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Kansas has one of the most narrow death penalties in the Nation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Woodman",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Eighth Amendment requires reliability in the determination that death is an appropriate sentence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Would it be constitutional if the Kansas statute said that, \"The jury must find, by a preponderance, that the aggravating factors outweigh the mitigating factors\"?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: May I ask you --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Woodman",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0002-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the jury can decree death without having made the individualized sentencing decision required at the selection stage."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: No, but --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. WOODMAN: Well, I mean, the Kansas Supreme Court determined that equipoise can happen. And in the situations that I've described, there is a real risk under this statute that the jury can either become agnostic and just pick, and the statute requires them to pick death, or the jury, encouraged by prosecutorial arguments to abdicate the decision, do abdicate, simply cop out and impose death, because it allows them to avoid making the tough choice."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Woodman",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0002-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Kansas Supreme Court determined that equipoise can happen."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1170",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d789a5f274dcdd208d6418c9e76a2f4256cfd9501964466e1f8bb0fc67f1569a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d789a5f274dc-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
